## 4. Authorization Architecture

Implement role-based access control (RBAC) at the backend and enforce resource ownership separately.

Create a centralized permission model instead of scattering role checks throughout the application.

Example conceptual permissions:

| Permission | Guest | Reader | Author | Admin |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Browse catalog | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Guest checkout | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| View own orders | No account history | Yes | Yes | Yes, authorized workflow |
| Manage own profile | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Submit author application | Optional public flow | Yes | Already approved | Admin workflow |
| Create book draft | No | No | Yes | Yes |
| Publish or moderate books | No | No | Only if authorized | Yes |
| Manage other users | No | No | No | Yes, authorized workflow |
| Assign administrator role | No | No | No | Protected provisioning only |

Treat this table as a starting point. Map permissions to the existing business logic and explicitly document any exceptions.

### Mandatory implementation rules

- Authenticate protected requests on the server.
- Authorize every protected API endpoint.
- Check resource ownership on every relevant read and write.
- Apply authorization to both frontend routes and backend APIs.
- Default to denying access when permissions are missing or ambiguous.
- Validate input schemas and resource identifiers.
- Prevent insecure direct object reference (IDOR) vulnerabilities.
- Prevent mass-assignment vulnerabilities by allowing only approved fields.
- Prevent privilege escalation and role tampering.
- Protect file uploads against unauthorized access and unsafe file types.
- Keep secrets in environment variables or the application's secret-management mechanism.
- Never expose private user, order, or administrative data through public API responses.
- Use transactions or equivalent consistency controls for sensitive multi-step operations.

Do not implement security by merely hiding menu items or redirecting users away from dashboards.

