## 12. Engineering Constraints

- Inspect the codebase before selecting libraries or changing architecture.
- Reuse existing components, services, database models, and design tokens wherever possible.
- Do not replace working features with mockups or placeholder pages.
- Do not hardcode credentials, API keys, secrets, or privileged accounts.
- Do not grant permissions based on frontend state.
- Do not weaken existing security to make a test pass.
- Do not remove existing content or redesign unrelated pages.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies or duplicate authentication systems.
- Do not use production data in automated tests.
- Do not simulate a successful implementation when the underlying functionality is absent.
- Keep changes modular, documented, maintainable, and testable.
- If a required external service is unavailable, document the dependency and provide a safe local testing alternative.

## Final instruction

Begin by auditing the existing repository and producing a concise implementation plan with the current stack, existing capabilities, gaps, and proposed changes.

Then implement the feature end to end, run the tests, fix the defects you find, and provide the evidence-based final report.

Prioritize correct permissions, guest shopping continuity, account security, and regression prevention over visual polish. The result must be a working, tested authentication and authorization system integrated into the existing bookstore—not merely a set of attractive login pages.
